    Videos-tab preview+controls with a small per-item-type dispatch, reusing existing backend workers and FS
    methods. Accept temporary UI-wiring duplication (removed later by the reusable-component extraction in the
    Roadmap) in exchange for NOT destabilizing the working Videos/Images tabs. Coexists with them (does not
    replace). Requires GUI click-testing (record/play/navigate across a mixed queue) that can't be done headless.

# Robustness / UX issues found in v2.3.0 field testing (2026-07)
[ ] HIGH: Handle inaccessible/slow source folders gracefully. Selecting a Google Drive folder whose
    contents were NOT available offline made the app HANG and eventually CRASH. Cause is almost
    certainly synchronous filesystem work on the UI thread: FolderAccessManager.is_accessible()/
    list_videos()/list_images() call os.listdir/os.path.isfile directly (vat/utils/fs_access.py), and
    cloud-backed placeholder files can block for a long time (or fail) while the provider hydrates them.
    Fixes to make:
    - Move folder scanning off the UI thread (worker + progress/cancel), or at minimum apply a timeout
      and show a "still loading / cancel" affordance instead of freezing.
    - Detect cloud placeholders on Windows (FILE_ATTRIBUTE_RECALL_ON_OPEN / RECALL_ON_DATA_ACCESS,
      also OFFLINE) and warn the user up front: "This folder is not available offline - make it
      available offline first, or copy it locally."
    - Never let a folder-load failure kill the app: catch, log, show a dialog, and leave the previous
      folder loaded.
[ ] Slow app startup, especially on lower-resource machines (ThinkPad, Parallels VM). Investigate:
    PyInstaller onefile self-extraction (~317 MB exe) is a prime suspect - a onedir build or trimming
    bundled deps would likely help more than app-level changes. Also profile import time (cv2, PySide6,
    pydub/numpy) and defer heavy imports until first use. NOTE: the refactor/modularization and the
    pywebview migration may both help here, but measure before assuming.
[ ] FEATURE: Allow compressed archives as source folders. In the folder picker, let the user select a
    .zip / .tar.gz / .tgz / .tar / (7z?) archive - or a sub-folder inside one - and have the app
    auto-extract it (in place, next to the archive, or to a cache dir) and then open the extracted
    folder as the working folder. Considerations: show extraction progress and allow cancel; handle
    archives whose media sits in a nested sub-folder (offer a picker or auto-descend when there is a
    single top-level dir); don't re-extract if already extracted (detect + reuse); make sure recordings
    are written to the EXTRACTED folder and warn that they live outside the archive; guard against
    path-traversal entries ("zip slip") and huge/zip-bomb archives; stdlib zipfile/tarfile cover
    zip/tar.gz so no new dependency is needed for the common cases.

# Refactoring / Modularization Roadmap (major — do AFTER the short-term "All" tab; ideally a dedicated
#   ultracode / sub-agent review session once usage resets, ideally Fable on Ultracode)
#
# GOAL: modularize the app into reusable components coded ONCE and used in multiple places, cutting the
# large-scale redundancy that built up across many earlier AI-assisted edits — WITHOUT losing behavior.
# HARD RULE: redundant-looking components often have real functional DIFFERENCES. Before merging any two,
# diff their behavior exhaustively and preserve EVERY distinct function. Verify feature-by-feature (ideally
# with GUI click-testing) before and after each merge. Reducing redundancy must never break a working path.
#
# Current state / why (measured):
#   - vat/ui/app.py is a 4,997-line God object (MainWindow, ~90 methods) — ~40% of the 12,341-line codebase.
#     It does theming, i18n, drawer, folder load, settings, ALL video handlers, ALL image handlers, audio
#     playback/recording orchestration, dialogs, export/clear/import/join, diagnostics, fullscreen, clipboard.
#   - vat/review/review_tab.py (1,645) and vat/i18n/builtin_labels.py (1,522) are the next-largest files.
#
# [ ] 1. WAV recording-naming redesign (foundational; enables robust All tab + same-stem kits).
#     Problem: stimulus kits may contain identically-named video+image files differing only by extension, and
#     the convert-to-MP4 feature can change a video's name. Current mapping:
#       - video  -> wav_path_for(): strips ext -> "<stem>.wav"       (clip01.mpg -> clip01.wav)
#       - image  -> wav_path_for_image(): keeps name -> "<full>.wav" (photo03.jpg -> photo03.jpg.wav)  <-- already uniform
#       - find_existing_image_audio() ALSO tries a stripped "<stem>.wav" fallback -> HAZARD: for image foo.jpg
#         it can return foo.wav (a same-stem VIDEO's recording) and play/DELETE it under the image.
#     Redesign (uniform + backward compatible):
#       (a) WRITE every recording as "<fullname>.wav" (videos join images in keeping the extension).
#       (b) READ for a video: prefer "<fullname>.wav", else fall back to legacy "<stem>.wav" (pre-refactor work;
#           i.e. a WAV with no embedded media extension maps to a video). Images already forward-compatible.
#       (c) convert-to-MP4 (_convert_current_video_in_place, vat/ui/app.py ~L2451): when foo.mpg -> foo.mp4,
#           RENAME the recording "<old>.wav" -> "<new>.wav" (source removal already happens via .bak.zip).
#       (d) Remove/scope the stripped-basename fallback in find_existing_image_audio so it cannot grab a
#           same-stem video's WAV. Reconcile every caller.
#       (e) Add unit tests for the mapping (video/image, legacy fallback, same-stem kit, convert migration).
#     Centralize all of this in FolderAccessManager (vat/utils/fs_access.py): collapse wav_path_for /
#     wav_path_for_image / find_existing_image_audio / has_image_audio into one media-type-aware API.
#
# [ ] 2. Extract a reusable MediaPane component (biggest redundancy win; unlocks the clean "All" tab).
#     The video and image media-item lifecycles are implemented TWICE in app.py (~16 twin pairs), differing
#     mainly in WAV naming and preview mechanism. Verify each pair's behavior, then merge into ONE component:
#       select        on_video_select            / on_image_select
#       preview        show_first_frame           / _load_image_pixmap
#       play audio     play_audio                 / play_image_audio
#       stop audio     stop_audio                 / stop_image_audio
#       record         toggle_recording           / toggle_image_recording
#       stop record    (video path)               / stop_image_recording
#       add audio file _handle_add_existing_audio_video / _handle_add_existing_audio_image
#       paste audio    _handle_paste_audio_video  / _handle_paste_audio_image
#       delete rec     _handle_delete_recording_video / _handle_delete_recording_image
#       edit ocenaudio _handle_edit_recording_ocenaudio_video / _handle_edit_recording_ocenaudio_image
#       copy clipboard _copy_current_video_to_clipboard / _copy_current_image_to_clipboard
#       save as        _save_current_video_as     / _save_current_image_as
#       context menu   _on_videos_context_menu    / _on_images_context_menu
#       fullscreen     _open_fullscreen_video     / _open_fullscreen_image
#       controls state update_media_controls      / _update_image_record_controls
#       folder-updated _on_videos_updated         / _on_images_updated
#     Give the component a DISPLAY-MODE strategy: single-preview/one-at-a-time (Videos, All) vs grid
#     (Images, Review). Then Videos / Images / All are all instances of it; the "All" tab stops being a copy.
#
# [ ] 3. AudioController — centralize playback/recording/join/convert worker lifecycle (currently scattered
#     on self with a spray of _on_*_thread_finished / _on_any_audio_finished handlers). This is where
#     cross-tab play/record state bugs would otherwise breed once a 3rd (All) pane shares the workers.
#
# [ ] 4. Split init_ui (~L635-1244, ~600 lines) into per-tab builder functions/classes.
#
# [ ] 5. Externalize i18n (builtin_labels.py, 1,522 lines of inline 7-language dict) to JSON/data files.
#     Cleanup AND a direct enabler for the pywebview/PWA port (the JS side wants JSON i18n anyway).
#
# [ ] 6. Review tab (review_tab.py, 1,645 lines): it HAS redundant media components (preview/playback) that
#     could share the MediaPane, BUT its purpose and behavior are very different (randomized quiz/review,
#     scoring/SFX, stats, grouped export). Treat its media bits as candidates for sharing the component while
#     PRESERVING all its distinct functionality — do the functional-diff audit here with extra care.
#
# [ ] 7. Small cleanups to fold in as areas are touched:
#     - De-duplicate generate_click_sound_pydub (exists in BOTH vat/ui/app.py ~L3959 AND vat/audio/joiner.py).
#     - Resolve the apparent DOUBLE eventFilter definition in MainWindow (app.py ~L1396 and ~L4094 — the second
#       silently overrides the first; confirm and remove the dead one).
#     - Tighten blanket "except Exception: pass" (26 in app.py) that swallow errors and hide field bugs
#       (e.g. the still-open playback segfault).
    - Fix undefined name in on_image_select's debug log (vat/ui/app.py: f-string references `exists`,
      which is not defined -> that log line never emits; silently swallowed by its try/except).

# Architecture: pywebview re-platform → PWA + native wrappers (major, future direction — AFTER the "All" tab above)
[ ] Re-platform the app: replace the PySide6/Qt GUI with a pywebview shell and move as much of
    the core functionality and UI/UX as possible into the HTML/CSS/JavaScript frontend. Keep
    Python only for essential native operations: filesystem/folder access, microphone recording,
    WAV concatenation/joining, ffmpeg/ffprobe invocation, and OS integration.
    Target shape:
    - Frontend (HTML/JS): all UI/UX — Videos & Images tabs, Review tab, playback controls,
      settings, metadata editing, and i18n (reuse the existing docs/ localization pattern:
      i18n IDs + per-language TRANSLATIONS + detectLanguage/localStorage across the 6 languages).
    - Python backend: exposed to JS via pywebview's `window.pywebview.api`. Owns FolderAccessManager
      (vat/utils/fs_access.py), recording (vat/audio/recording.py — 24-bit/48 kHz PCM), joining
      (vat/audio/joiner.py), and ffmpeg resolution/config (vat/utils/resources.py).
    - PWA build: a pure-web variant (no Python) that installs/runs in a browser, mirroring what was
      done for the flextext editor suite. Aim for interoperability / interconnection with that suite
      (shared file-naming conventions, data formats, possibly cross-linking/hand-off between apps).
      CAVEAT: a browser PWA CANNOT produce archive-compliant recordings — MediaRecorder / Web Audio
      give compressed (WebM/Opus) or non-guaranteed WAV, not uncompressed 24-bit/48 kHz PCM. Position
      the PWA as a lightweight data-collection tool for contexts where a web platform is needed, and
      point users to the native app for archival capture. (Reflect this in the FAQ if/when shipped.)
    - Native wrappers:
        (a) Windows — pywebview as the native desktop wrapper (replacing/alongside the current
            PyInstaller packaging); native Python recording keeps archival quality.
        (b) Android — a native wrapper (e.g. WebView / Trusted Web Activity around the PWA) using
            native Android audio APIs for better capture quality than a plain browser PWA.
    Migration considerations / risks:
    - Large rewrite; must preserve feature parity — Review tab logic & randomization, Export Sets /
      Export Results, YAML settings + export, click-separated joins, Ocenaudio integration,
      overwrite-confirm + auto-save UX, image-vs-video thumbnail handling.
    - Pick the frontend stack (vanilla vs. framework) and SHARE one UI codebase between the pywebview
      desktop build and the standalone PWA to avoid maintaining two UIs; abstract the native calls
      behind a small interface (pywebview API on desktop, browser/File System Access API + limited
      capture on web) so the same frontend targets both.
    - Define the Python<->JS API surface once and reuse it across desktop and (partially) Android.
